s and 4 seasons). 16 held up after correcting for running many tests at once (false discovery rate q = 0.05); most of them repeat the main trend month by month. Findings marked “follow-up check” come from these checks."},"deeper_checks":null,"provenance":{"dataset":"NASA/DLR GRACE and NASA/GFZ GRACE-FO (JPL mascons) · lwe_thickness","version":"RL06.3Mv04","doi":"10.5067/TEMSC-3JC634","landing_page":"https://podaac.jpl.nasa.gov/dataset/TELLUS_GRAC-GRFO_MASCON_CRI_GRID_RL06.3_V4","accessed":"2026-11-14","processing":["converted longitudes 0..360 to -180..180","kept land cells only (product land_mask == 1)","region value = mean of the 2° map squares that overlap the region's outline, weighted by the overlapping area","monthly series 2003-01 to 2025-12 (complete years)","anomalies from the calendar-month normal; Sen's slope, Mann-Kendall (Yue-Wang), OLS with effective sample size (trend_stats.py)"],"synthetic":false,"caveat":"Data: NASA/DLR GRACE and NASA/GFZ GRACE-FO satellites, which weigh the water on land by its gravity; JPL mascon solution RL06.3, blocks of about 300 km. The trend is a straight line through 2003–2025; it shows what changed, not why. The record is 23 years long, shorter than the 30 years usually used to describe a climate. GRACE weighs all water together (it cannot say whether groundwater, soil or snow changed), sees blocks of about 300 km, and has no data from mid-2017 to mid-2018 between the two missions; months without data are left out, never filled."}}